  "spans": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "nodeId": "n1",
      "text": "Google X Head on Moonshots: 10X Is Easier Than 10 Percent.",
      "contextBefore": "",
      "contextAfter": " Here is the surprising truth: It's often easier to make something 10 times better than it is to make it 10 percent better. Yes ... really. Because when you're working to make things 10 percent better",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 58,
      "extractSha256": "826820a0b22f88539e5907edae91f21dac539deafa781d10d1231b0f3f507813",
      "windowSha256": "655075e6cc88a23688d6da5638ec0a0602aa1a24175572d0b1dc164fa392d1a9",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-03T02:33:34.642Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20131203110232/https://www.wired.com/2013/02/moonshots-matter-heres-how-to-make-them-happen",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "nodeId": "n2",
      "text": "In this issue, \"we\" refers to our collective judgment.* A commonly-cited claim is that powering an individual ChatGPT query requires around 3 watt-hours of electricity, or 10 times as much as a Google search.1 This is often brought up to express concern over AI's impact on the environment, climate change, or the electric grid.",
      "contextBefore": "ts solely represent the views of the authors, and do not necessarily reflect the views of Epoch AI as a whole. *Credit to Alex Erben and Ege Erdil for substantial help with research and calculations. ",
      "contextAfter": " However, we believe that this figure of 3 watt-hours per query is likely an overestimate. In this issue, we revisit this question using a similar methodology, but using up-to-date facts and clearer a",
      "start": 495,
      "end": 823,
      "extractSha256": "1941ca221ac31355b9ec40c74fecea4d2d790eaa7776b2a0b36d0113c1492e61",
      "windowSha256": "e745f5971b9d10b4571c5bd6ead28926120756fa208619a19cfe0061eb7cdac0",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-03T02:33:35.573Z",
